**Finding (Correctness — Required):** Calling `rows.sort(...)` directly mutates the shared input array in place, corrupting other consumers. Additionally, returning `-1` for equal `time` values breaks comparator symmetry and relative order on ties.

**Scope & Checks:** Static review of the snippet only; no build or unit tests were executed.

**Correction:**
```ts
const sortedRows = rows.toSorted((a, b) => a.time - b.time);
```

**Regression Example:**
```ts
import assert from "node:assert/strict";

type EventRow = { id: number; time: number };

function getSortedRows(rows: readonly EventRow[]): EventRow[] {
  return rows.toSorted((a, b) => a.time - b.time);
}

const sharedRows: EventRow[] = [
  { id: 1, time: 200 },
  { id: 2, time: 100 },
  { id: 3, time: 100 },
];

const sorted = getSortedRows(sharedRows);
assert.notEqual(sorted, sharedRows);
assert.deepEqual(sharedRows.map((r) => r.id), [1, 2, 3]);
assert.deepEqual(sorted.map((r) => r.id), [2, 3, 1]);
```
